PAGES=[page('116_page_2.jpg',224,[eq(r'\frac{2\sqrt{a^2(1+m^2)-c^2}}{\sqrt{1+m^2}}=0'),eq(r'\sqrt{a^2(1+m^2)-c^2}=0,\quad\mathrm{squaring\ both\ sides}'),eq(r'a^2(1+m^2)-c^2=0\ \Rightarrow\ c^2=a^2(1+m^2)\ \Rightarrow\ c=\pm a\sqrt{1+m^2}','ii'),paragraph('The equation (ii) is the required condition at which the line y = mx+c touches the circle x²+y² = a².'),
 heading('Example 8'),paragraph('Find the length of the chord joining the points P and Q on the line x/a+y/b = 1 which cuts the circle x²+y² = r². Show that if the line touches the circle, then a⁻²+b⁻² = r⁻².'),heading('Solution'),paragraph('The slope of a given line x/a+y/b = 1:'),eq(r'\frac yb=-\frac xa+1\ \Rightarrow\ y=-\frac bax+b,\quad m=-\frac ba'),paragraph('If PQ is the chord of a circle x²+y² = r², and PQ is 2 times of MP, then the length of chord PQ through result (i) is:'),eq(r'|PQ|=2|OM|=2\sqrt{\frac{a^2(1+m^2)-c^2}{1+m^2}}'),eq(r'=2\sqrt{\frac{r^2[1+(-b/a)^2]-b^2}{1+(-b/a)^2}},\quad a^2=r^2,m=-\frac ba,c=b'),paragraph('If the given line touches the circle x²+y² = r², then the length of the chord PQ is going to be zero:'),eq(r'2\sqrt{\frac{r^2[1+(-b/a)^2]-b^2}{1+(-b/a)^2}}=0'),eq(r'4\frac{r^2[1+(-b/a)^2]-b^2}{1+(-b/a)^2}=0,\quad\mathrm{squaring\ both\ sides}'),eq(r'r^2[1+(-b/a)^2]-b^2=0'),eq(r'b^2=r^2\left[1+\frac{b^2}{a^2}\right]'),note('Here a,b are the nonzero axis intercepts and r > 0 is the radius. The initial generic circle formula reuses a for a radius before replacing it with r; it does not assert that the intercept a equals r. The paragraph correctly says PQ = 2MP, but the equation repeats the erroneous 2OM label. The chord length simplifies to 2√(r²−a²b²/(a²+b²)); a real secant requires a²b²/(a²+b²) < r².')]),
 page('117_page_1.jpg',225,[eq(r'b^2=r^2\frac{a^2+b^2}{a^2}\ \Rightarrow\ \frac{a^2b^2}{a^2+b^2}=r^2\ \Rightarrow\ r^{-2}=\frac{a^2+b^2}{a^2b^2}=\frac1{a^2}+\frac1{b^2}=a^{-2}+b^{-2}'),
 heading('Example 9'),paragraph('Find the coordinates of the middle point of the chord which the circle x²+y²+4x−2y−3 = 0 cuts off on the line x−y+2 = 0.'),heading('Solution'),paragraph('The center of the given circle is C(−g,−f) = C(−2,1) and the line x−y+2 = 0 (line AB) intersects the circle at points P and Q and M(x₁,y₁) is the middle point of the chord PQ. Join C and M that develops a line CM perpendicular to chord PQ.'),MIDPOINT,paragraph('If M lies on line AB, then the line equation x−y+2 = 0 becomes:'),eq(r'x_1-y_1+2=0','i'),paragraph('The slopes of the lines (i) and CM are respectively:'),eq(r'm_2=1,\quad\mathrm{coefficient\ of}\ x'),eq(r'm_1=\frac{y_1-1}{x_1+2},\quad\mathrm{slope\ of}\ CM'),paragraph('If CM is perpendicular to AB, then the product of their slopes equals −1:'),eq(r'\left(\frac{y_1-1}{x_1+2}\right)(1)=-1\ \Rightarrow\ y_1-1=-x_1-2\ \Rightarrow\ x_1+y_1+1=0','ii'),paragraph('The equations (i) and (ii) are solved to obtain the coordinates of the middle point M:'),eq(r'x_1-y_1+2=0,\quad x_1+y_1+1=0\ \Rightarrow\ x_1=-\frac32,y_1=\frac12'),paragraph('Thus, the coordinates of the middle point is M(−3/2,1/2).'),
 heading('8.3.3 The equation of a tangent to a circle in slope form'),paragraph('If m is the slope of the tangent to the circle'),eq(r'x^2+y^2=a^2','i'),paragraph('then the equation of tangent line is of the form'),eq(r'y=mx+c','ii'),paragraph('Here c is to be calculated from the fact that the line (ii) is tangent to the circle (i). The line (ii) is used in circle (i) to obtain the quadratic equation in x:'),eq(r'x^2+(mx+c)^2=a^2,\quad y=mx+c'),eq(r'x^2(1+m^2)+2mcx+(c^2-a^2)=0','iii'),paragraph('If the line (ii) touches the circle (i), then the quadratic equation (iii) has coincident roots for which the discriminate of the quadratic equation (iii) equals zero:'),eq(r'\mathrm{Disc}=0'),eq(r'4m^2c^2-4(1+m^2)(c^2-a^2)=0'),eq(r'4m^2c^2=4(1+m^2)(c^2-a^2)'),eq(r'm^2c^2=m^2c^2+c^2-a^2-a^2m^2'),eq(r'c^2=a^2(1+m^2)\ \Rightarrow\ c=\pm a\sqrt{1+m^2}','iv')]),
 page('117_page_2.jpg',226,[paragraph('Equation (iv) is the condition of tangency. The value of c from equation (iv) is used in the line (ii) to obtain the required equation of the tangent:'),eq(r'y=mx+c=mx\pm a\sqrt{1+m^2}','v'),heading('Remember'),paragraph('The equation of any tangent to the circle x²+y² = a² in the slope form is:'),eq(r'y=mx\pm a\sqrt{1+m^2}','vi'),paragraph('The line y = mx+c should touch the circle x²+y² = a² under condition:'),eq(r'c=\pm a\sqrt{1+m^2}','vii'),paragraph('The interpretation of result (v) is that the line x+my+n = 0 should touch the circle x²+y² = a² under condition:'),eq(r'a^2(1^2+m^2)-n^2=0\ \Rightarrow\ n=\pm a\sqrt{1^2+m^2}','viii'),paragraph('The interpretation of result (v) that the line x+my+n = 0 should touch the circle x²+y²+2gx+2fy+c = 0 under condition:'),eq(r'(c-f^2)^2+2fgm+(c-g^2)m^2-2n(g+fm)+n^2=0','ix'),
 paragraph('Let y = mx+a√(1+m²) be a tangent to a circle (i) at a point (x₁,y₁), if the circle equation (i) is identical to xx₁+yy₁ = a², then the coefficients of like terms of y = mx+a√(1+m²) and xx₁+yy₁ = a² ⇒ yy₁ = −xx₁+a² are compared to obtain the point of contact:'),eq(r'\frac{x_1}{-m}=\frac{y_1}1=\frac{a^2}{a\sqrt{1+m^2}}'),eq(r'\frac{x_1}{-m}=\frac a{\sqrt{1+m^2}}\ \Rightarrow\ x_1=\frac{-am}{\sqrt{1+m^2}}'),eq(r'\frac{y_1}1=\frac a{\sqrt{1+m^2}}\ \Rightarrow\ y_1=\frac a{\sqrt{1+m^2}}'),eq(r'(x_1,y_1)=\left(\frac{-am}{\sqrt{1+m^2}},\frac a{\sqrt{1+m^2}}\right)','x'),
 note('The circle equation and its tangent equation are not identical curves: xx₁+yy₁ = a² is the tangent at a circumference point. Comparing coefficients of the two tangent equations gives the displayed positive-intercept contact point. The negative branch has the negative of that point. For m = 0 the division by −m must be replaced by a direct coefficient comparison. In (ix) the extra square on (c−f²) is incorrect. The distance condition is (n−g−fm)² = (g²+f²−c)(1+m²), which expands to (c−f²)+2fgm+(c−g²)m²−2n(g+fm)+n² = 0. Require g²+f²−c > 0 for a genuine circle.'),
 heading('Example 10'),paragraph('For what value of c, the line x+y+c = 0 will touch the circle x²+y² = 64? Use that value of c to find the tangent that should touch the given circle. Find also the contact point.'),heading('Solution'),paragraph('The slope of the line x+y+c = 0 is m = −1. The value of c at which the line will touch the circle x²+y² = 64 is:'),eq(r'c=\pm a\sqrt{1+m^2}=\pm8\sqrt{1+(-1)^2}=8\sqrt2,\quad a=8,m=-1'),paragraph('The required tangent line that should touch the given circle is:'),eq(r'y=mx\pm a\sqrt{1+m^2}=-x\pm8\sqrt2'),paragraph('The point of contact through result (x) is:'),eq(r'(x_1,y_1)=\left(\frac{-am}{\sqrt{1+m^2}},\frac a{\sqrt{1+m^2}}\right)=\left(\frac8{\sqrt2},\frac8{\sqrt2}\right)'),note('The source drops ± in the last c equality and supplies only the positive-intercept tangent’s contact. Both constants c = ±8√2 are valid in x+y+c = 0; their contacts are (−c/2,−c/2). The symbol c in y = mx+c is the negative of the constant c in x+y+c = 0, so the two roles must be distinguished.'),
 heading('8.3.4 The equations of tangent and normal to a circle at a point'),paragraph('The equation of a circle is:'),eq(r'x^2+y^2+2gx+2fy+c=0','i'),paragraph('If A(x₁,y₁) is a point lying on the circle (i), then the circle (i) becomes:'),eq(r'x_1^2+y_1^2+2gx_1+2fy_1+c=0','ii')])]
